course: dictionary
lesson: 1886
title: Collide: crash or clash
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To collide means to crash into something or someone while you are moving. «Two cars collided.» Both cars were moving, and they hit each other. Collide is a verb, so its forms change: collides, collided, colliding.
explain: The word can also describe ideas. If plans or opinions collide, they clash and strongly disagree. The words around it give you the clue: is something being hit, or are people arguing?
explain: Context helps here too. «When plates collide, layers of rock can be bent into folds» tells you collide is about big things pushing hard against each other. Try the swap: put "crash into" in the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: To collide means to crash into something while moving.
fact: «Collided» is the past tense of collide: «Two cars collided.»
fact: If ideas, plans or opinions collide, they clash and strongly disagree.
fact: «Collide with» is a common phrase, and a «head-on collision» is a crash where two things hit front to front.
fact: Faster and heavier objects hit harder when they collide, so players in PE should watch out so they don't collide.
exercise: «Two cars collided.» What does collided mean? | Stopped at a red light | Parked side by side | *Crashed into each other while moving | Driven slowly past each other
exercise: Nadia and Ben keep arguing because their plans collide. What does collide suggest? | They agree completely | They are going to the same party | They are both asleep | *They strongly disagree
exercise: «Sea fossils are found at the top of Mount Everest, because that rock formed under the sea and was pushed up as continents collided.» Find and copy the word that is a form of collide. | Everest | *collided | fossils | formed
test: «A girl played on a trampoline with no net and another child jumping too; they collided and she broke her leg.» What happened? | The children waved at each other | *The two children crashed into each other | The trampoline was moved to a new garden | They played a quiet game
test: What does «collide with» mean? | Agree with the rules | Meet a friend by chance | Speak very quietly | *Crash into something while moving
exam: Which sentence uses collide to mean a clash of ideas? | *Their plans collided, and the two friends argued all afternoon | The ball collided with the wall | The two trains collided at the station | The footballers collided during training
